+# Block placement performance review
+
+Covers `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`, going through a list of suggested
+optimizations.
+
+## The load we're dealing with
+
+- The server rate-limits each player to one placement per 0.1 s (`MIN_INTERVAL`), so at most 10
+  per second per player.
+- A server holds 8 players, so the most it can see is **80 placements per second**, and that only
+  if every player is spam-clicking at once.
+- On the client, work only happens on a click or tap: a few per second at most.
+- Nobody has reported lag, and nobody has profiled this code and found it to be a hotspot.
+
+At this volume, each handler call costs a few microseconds of Luau plus one part creation. That
+works out to well under 1% of a frame even at the worst-case rate. None of the items below changes
+anything a player can notice. So the real question for each one is whether it costs complexity or
+correctness, and whether it is worth doing for some reason other than speed.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+| # | Suggestion | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Pool block Parts | **Rejected.** Nothing to recycle |
+| 2 | Pack arguments into a buffer | **Rejected.** Saves almost nothing, costs validation clarity |
+| 3 | Switch to UnreliableRemoteEvent | **Rejected.** Would drop players' placements |
+| 4 | `--!native` on both scripts | **Rejected.** No hot code for it to speed up |
+| 5 | Lookup table instead of `table.find` | **Done**, as a simplification, not for speed |
+| 6 | Create RaycastParams once | **Rejected.** Saves nothing measurable, and adds respawn bookkeeping |
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts: rejected
+
+Pooling pays off when objects are created and destroyed over and over: projectiles, effects,
+debris. Placed blocks are **never destroyed**. Nothing in the codebase removes them, so a pool
+would never get a part back to reuse. Every placement would still need a new part. A pool built
+in advance would just be thousands of parts sitting in memory before anyone needed them, and
+`Instance.new` plus setting a few properties is cheap at 80 per second at most. If block removal
+gets added later, and profiling shows that create/destroy churn costs something, revisit it then.
+
+### 2. Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer: rejected
+
+Each event carries a short string (the name, at most 6 characters) and a Vector3. Roblox already
+serializes this compactly: the whole payload is a couple dozen bytes. At 10 events per second per
+player that is a few hundred bytes per second, nowhere near the network budget. Packing the data
+into a buffer could save maybe ten bytes per event. The cost is hand-written encode and decode on
+both sides, plus more server validation. The server would have to check the buffer's length and
+read values out of it safely before it could do the `typeof` and NaN checks it does now. That's
+more code on the trust boundary for no benefit anyone can see. Buffers make sense for
+high-frequency, high-volume data like per-frame state replication, not for clicks.
+
+### 3. Make PlaceBlock an UnreliableRemoteEvent: rejected (would cause bugs)
+
+Unreliable remotes aren't "faster" in any way that matters here. What they do is skip
+retransmission and ordering, so **packets can be dropped or arrive out of order**. They're meant
+for data where the next update replaces the last one, like positions or cosmetic effects. A
+placement is a one-off action: if the packet is lost, the player clicked and no block appeared,
+with no error. The client would see random failed placements, especially on poor connections. The
+latency reliable delivery adds is unnoticeable for a click.
+
+### 4. Add `--!native` to both scripts: rejected
+
+Native code generation speeds up long-running, compute-heavy Luau: tight math loops, procedural
+generation, pathfinding grids. These scripts are event handlers that run a few lines of checks and
+then call into the engine (`Instance.new`, setting properties, `Raycast`, `FireServer`). Native
+codegen does nothing for time spent inside engine calls. The bit of pure Luau here runs at most 80
+times a second, so there's nothing to win, and native compilation uses extra memory. Save
+`--!native` for code that profiling shows is CPU-bound in Luau.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table: done, as a simplification
+
+The speed argument doesn't hold up. `table.find` over 5 names takes nanoseconds. But the change is
+still worth making, because the lookup table **already exists**. The server built a separate
+`VALID_NAMES` array just to call `table.find` on it, then looked the name up in
+`BlockTypes.ByName` on the next line anyway. Now it checks `BlockTypes.ByName[blockTypeName]`
+directly and returns if that's nil. That's one source of truth instead of two, and less code.
+Behavior doesn't change: `ByName` is a plain frozen table with no metatable, and the earlier
+`typeof(blockTypeName) ~= "string"` check means only string keys ever reach it.
+
+The client's `table.find(NUMBER_KEYS, input.KeyCode)` runs once per key press over 5 entries and
+reads clearly as "which hotbar slot". Left as is.
+
+### 6. Create the RaycastParams once: rejected
+
+`RaycastParams.new()` runs once per click, which costs nothing measurable. The current code also
+has a real advantage: it reads `Players.LocalPlayer.Character` fresh on every click, so the filter
+is always correct after a respawn. A cached `RaycastParams` would need its
+`FilterDescendantsInstances` updated on `CharacterAdded`. Otherwise, after the first death, rays
+would hit your own new character and you'd place blocks on yourself. That extra bookkeeping is
+where bugs come from, and the gain is invisible. This one becomes worth doing if raycasting moves
+into a per-frame loop, such as a placement preview ghost that follows the mouse.
+
+## If performance ever does become an issue
+
+Profile first, using the MicroProfiler and the Developer Console's Script Performance and
+Network tabs. The likeliest long-term cost isn't this code at all. It's the **total number of
+placed parts**, since blocks pile up forever, which affects physics, rendering and replication.
+If big builds start to lag, the levers are things like a cap on blocks per server, merging blocks,
+or streaming. None of them are on this list.
